<!-- anchor:frontmatter-empty-value-rule -->
**frontmatter notation — no `#` · the empty value is `None`**

In frontmatter no PK takes a `#` (in YAML `#` starts a comment): attempt `7`, lesson `K003`. In the body, `#7`, `#K003`.

A normally empty ID slot is unified as **`None`, whatever the location**. frontmatter is no exception and `null` is not used (e.g. `best_attempt: None`, `archived_through: None`). "Unfilled (violation)" is `—` alone.
- Check rule (no branching by location, a total function): pass ⟺ (`PK regex match` ∨ `value == None`). The whole complement is a violation — `value == —` is an unfilled violation (required field), any other value is a regex mismatch violation. (e.g. `best_attempt: 7`·`None` → pass / `best_attempt: —` → unfilled violation / `best_attempt: no7` → regex violation)
- The only branch forced on the parser is the `#` drop described in this block — it is a prefix matter, orthogonal to the empty-value token
- ⚠ Scope: applies **only to single-value ID fields**. The empty value of a list (`attempts`) stays `[]`, and of an integer (`backtracks`) stays `0` (each keeps its native empty value)
<!-- anchor-end:frontmatter-empty-value-rule -->

