<!-- anchor:check-execution -->
**Running checks and lookups — `.claude/hooks/nav.js`**

The script does invariant checks and index value lookups. A command runs the command line and follows the output as it is. It does not recompute or rewrite the verdict values and values of the output.

| Command (appended after `node .claude/hooks/nav.js`) | Output |
|---|---|
| `check` | The verdict block of all items of the three invariant tables |
| `check --c3` | The verdict block of I-01 · I-07 · I-08 · I-09 |
| `status` | 3 lines: `[Current position]` · `[Status]` · `[Do-not-retry directions]` |
| `state {Attempt PK}` | 4 lines: `Attempt:` · `Result:` · `Conclusion:` · `Status:` |
| `brief` or `brief {session file path} {round number}` | `[Path]` · `[Current position]` · `[Destination criterion]` · `[Best Record]` · `[Discard check candidates]` · `[Valid lessons]` · `[Next Direction Candidates]` · `[Open cycle]` · `[Resume point]` |
| `last {Attempt PK}` | The `[Last round]` block or `[Last round] None` |
| `onpath {Attempt PK}` | 5 lines: `Path:` · `EndNode:` · `Contains:` · `RowBranchPoint:` · `ChainPath:` |
| `route` | `Today` · `Now` · `LatestSessionPath` · `TodayFileExists` · `RoundSet` · `OpenRoundCount` · `OpenRound` (0 or more lines) · `NextRound` · `ResumableAttempts` · `NextAttemptNumber` |
| `dest --create` | When `00_destination.md` does not exist, creates the file by writing the structure template character for character and prints one line `Destination file created: …`. `[nav.js error]` when the file already exists |
| `dest` | `Today:` · `DestinationFile:` · `DestinationStatus:` · `Final Goal:` · `Criterion:` (0 or more lines) · `NextCriterionNumber:` · the I-21 item line · the I-22 item line |
| `recover` | `Marker: None`, or per marker the `Session` and `Target` lines and a final `Incomplete:` line |
| `snapshot` | Records the pre-write state in `locks/wrap-snapshot.json`, and appends at the end of `history/YYYY-MM.log` (the month of today) the full text of each of `00_destination.md`·`01_exploration_tree.md`·`02_knowledge_base.md` that differs by even one character from its latest history entry. Appends nothing when no file differs |
| `history` | One line per history entry `NNNN \| YYYY-MM-DD \| {list of files written in that entry}` (ascending numbers) or `History: None` |
| `diff {number} {number}` | The line-level difference of two history entries. When the second number is omitted, the difference between the first entry and the current files. `- ` = a line only on the earlier side, `+ ` = a line only on the later side |
| `anchor {key}` | The original text of an anchor block of this file |

- An item line of a verdict block = ` I-NN  {verdict value}  {value read}`, and a `❌` gets `expected:` on the next line. Verdict values → grep -F "anchor:verdict-values" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
- The last two lines of a verdict block = `Result: pass a / violation b / undetermined c / skipped d` · `IsolationSet: {I-NN in ascending order | None}`. IsolationSet = the `❌` items (with `--c3`, the `❌` and `⚠` items).
- A `[Notice]` line = an alert threshold was exceeded; it is not a violation. Alert thresholds (3, in this order): `01_exploration_tree.md line count` > 150 · `path node count` > 8 · `lesson list row count` > 40. A command only reports them and does not delete or shorten anything.
- Output starting with `[nav.js error]` = the run failed. The command stops and shows that line to the user. These are also `[nav.js error]`: a command not in the table · a `check` or `dest` argument not in the table · a command that reads `01_exploration_tree.md` when that file does not exist · `recover` when `locks/` holds a `.lock` file that is not `invalidation.lock` · an invalidation marker line that fits none of the `Session:` line · the `Invalidation target:` line · the target line regex during `recover`.
- An item whose check raised a script exception is `❌`, and `error during check: {message}` comes in the place of the value read (it is in the IsolationSet).
- On a violation: propose a fix, and do not edit a file without user approval.
- Invariant definitions: T1 invariants → grep -F "anchor:invariant-table-T1" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS) · T2 invariants → grep -F "anchor:invariant-table-T2" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS) · T3 invariants → grep -F "anchor:invariant-table-T3" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS) · run condition per tier → grep -F "anchor:invariant-tiers" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
- `state` output: `Result:`·`Conclusion:` = the values of the latest closed round of that attempt (`None` when there is no closed round), `Status:` = the computed status. When it cannot be computed, `Undetermined — {reason}` comes in the place of the value.
- The status of `status`·`brief` = `{status} (Attempt {Attempt PK})`. Target attempt = when there is an open round, in `route` the first `OpenRound` line attempt (then ` · open Round N` is added inside the parentheses); otherwise ★ `Attempt #:`.
- `dest` output: `DestinationFile` = `Present` or `None`. `DestinationStatus` = `Unwritten` (the file does not exist or equals the structure template line by line) / `Written` (I-21 is `✅` and I-22 is not `❌`) / `FormatViolation` (anything else). `Final Goal:` = that line when the section has exactly 1 value line, otherwise `None`. A `Criterion:` line = `Criterion: {D#} | {[ ] or [x]} | {Quantitative or Qualitative} | {In effect value} | {Recorded or Unrecorded} | {wording}`. Structure template → grep -F "anchor:00-file-structure" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
- The `[Best Record]` line of `brief` = `{Closest attempt} | {Why closest} | What blocked it: {What blocked it}`, copying the values of the ◎ Best Record code block of `01_exploration_tree.md` as they are. `None` when `Closest attempt` is `—`.
- `Now` of `route` = the run time `HH:MM` (24-hour). `OpenRoundCount` and the `OpenRound` lines are counted across all session files. An `OpenRound` line = `OpenRound: Round {N} · Attempt {Attempt PK}{ continued} · {session file path}` (a file with a later date comes first, and inside one file a lower round comes first. Round numbers grow downward inside a file).
- `[Open cycle]` of `brief` = `None` when there is no open round. Otherwise the target round (with arguments, that open round of that session file; without arguments, the round of the first `OpenRound` line of `route`. `[nav.js error]` when the arguments point to no open round) is written as `Round {N} · Attempt {Attempt PK} · {session file path}`, and below it, indented and copied character for character from that round block: 1 `Goal:` line · the `Hypothesis:` lines · the `Success Criteria:` lines · `DO: {entry count}` with the list of DO entry headers · 1 `PLAN: {Written | Unwritten}` line. `Unwritten` = among the five PLAN slots of that round (`Cycle goal:` · `Hypothesis:` · `Success criteria (this cycle):` · `Lessons consulted:` · `Discard check:`) there is a slot whose value is `—` or that has no value line. `Written` = anything else.
- `last` output: target = the very last round, in session file and round order, among the closed rounds of that attempt (the same round `state` reads). `[Last round] None` when there is no closed round. Otherwise, below `[Last round] Round {N} · Attempt {Attempt PK} · {session file path}`, indented and copied character for character from that round block: `Goal:` · the `Hypothesis:` lines · the `Success Criteria:` lines · `DO: {entry count}` with the list of DO entry headers · the `Success criteria met:` lines · `Distance to destination:` · `Conclusion:` · the `Reason:` lines.
- `onpath` output: `Path:` = the ★ `Path:` value. `EndNode:` = the last node of that path. `Contains:` = `Yes` when the argument is a node of that path, otherwise `No`. `RowBranchPoint:` = the `Branch point` value of the row whose `#` is the argument, in Recent Attempts and in the Migrated Attempt Summaries of every `archive/tree_YYYY-MM.md` (`None` when there is no such row). `ChainPath:` = the node sequence walked back from the argument to the `Branch point` of its attempt row, then the `Branch point` of that attempt row, and so on down to `#0`, written from `#0` in path format (`#0` when the argument is `#0`). `None` when, on the way back, some attempt does not have exactly 1 row, or a `Branch point` is not in {Attempt PK} format or is not below the `#` of its row. `[nav.js error]` when the argument has 2 or more rows or the ★ path cannot be read as nodes.
- `[Discard check candidates]` of `brief` = the rows of the Discarded Directions table whose `Branch point` is in the check node set. Check node set = ★ `Path` nodes ∪ the chain path nodes of the attempt of the target open round (only the ★ `Path` nodes when there is no open round or the chain path is `None`). Header line = `[Discard check candidates] {row count} (Branch point ∈ Path)`, and when the chain path added a node outside the ★ path the parentheses hold `Branch point ∈ Path ∪ Attempt {Attempt PK} ChainPath {chain path}`. The check node set of I-15 is built the same way from the attempt of the first `OpenRound` line of `route`.
- `[Resume point]` of `brief` = `Did: {value} | Remaining: {value} | Next first action: {value}` when the last DO entry of that round is a well-formed session-pause memo, otherwise `None`.
- `NextAttemptNumber` = the greatest attempt number among the tree, the archives and the round headers of all session files, + 1 (no reuse).
- `ResumableAttempts` = the attempt numbers found in the ★ `Path` nodes of `01_exploration_tree.md` · Recent Attempts `#` · every `archive/tree_YYYY-MM.md` Migrated Attempt Summaries `#`, minus `#0` and the numbers in the `#` column of the Discarded Directions table (ascending, `None` when empty). Item = `{Attempt PK} ({computed status})`, joined with `, `. The computed status is the same value as `Status:` of `state`, and when it cannot be computed the parentheses hold `Undetermined`. `#0` is the state before the first attempt, so it is not an attempt to resume.
<!-- anchor-end:check-execution -->

<!-- anchor:invalidation-write-order -->
**Invalidation transaction — write order**

A lesson invalidation is written in 3 places: index, body and tree. It is written only in the order below.

1. Create `locks/invalidation.lock` — record the target lessons and reasons first. Invalidation marker format → grep -F "anchor:marker-grammar" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
2. Set `Validity` of the target row of the index `02_knowledge_base.md` to `Invalid`
3. Fill in `Invalidation:` of the body `knowledge/K###.md`. Invalidation value format → grep -F "anchor:composite-invalidation" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
4. Fill in the Return Log row of the tree `01_exploration_tree.md` (including the `Invalidated lessons` column)
5. Delete `locks/invalidation.lock`

- Each step is skipped when it is already applied.
- A remaining marker means the last invalidation was interrupted. The next `/pdca-start` runs 2~5 again with the targets and reasons of the `recover` output. The targets and reasons are not judged again.
- Neither the index row nor the body file is deleted.
<!-- anchor-end:invalidation-write-order -->

<!-- anchor:marker-grammar -->
**Marker grammar — `locks/invalidation.lock`** (control file of the invalidation transaction)

    Session: 2026-07-29
    Invalidation target:
      #K003 — the fixed-driver premise collapsed with the return to #4
      #K007 — unnecessary after the higher goal changed

- File form = **plain text** (no YAML frontmatter). It has exactly two sections: 1 `Session:` line + the `Invalidation target:` section (1 or more target lines).
- `Session:` value = 1 session PK.
- For each target line of the invalidation target section, the target line regex (exact match): `^{Lesson PK} — .+$`. Left = lesson PK, right = reason prose (must not be blank).
- The PK formats of this marker (`YYYY-MM-DD` of the `Session` value, `#K###` on the left of a target line) are not newly defined but referenced → grep -F "anchor:pk-definition-table" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS).
- Target lines are **ascending, fixed** — the format lists 1 target per line, so its ordering rule is separate from columns that put several comma-separated values in one cell. For stable diffs and recovery comparison.
- Valid example: `#K003 — driver premise collapsed` · Violation examples: `#K3 — reason` (not three digits) · `#K003` (no ` — reason`) · `#K003 —` (blank reason)
- This block sets the **format** only.
<!-- anchor-end:marker-grammar -->

<!-- anchor:integrity-hook-marker -->
**Marker grammar — `locks/integrity-check.pending`** (gate of the integrity check hook)

- File form = **empty file** (no content). The test is **existence only** — when it exists, "now is the time to check"; when absent, the hook is a no-op. No content parsing.
- Existence test (exact match): true/false of `test -f locks/integrity-check.pending`.
- Naming: `locks/` lowercase plain (same shape as archive·knowledge·sessions). The extension `.pending` is **fixed** — with `.lock`, `nav.js recover` would read it as an invalidation marker, so `.lock` is **forbidden**.
- It is a **separate file with a separate purpose** from the invalidation marker (`locks/invalidation.lock`): the invalidation marker = a durable record of intent written **right before** the writes · deleted by wrap/start (for recovery). This marker = created **after all** writes are done · deleted by the hook (the check-time gate). Creation time and the deleting party are opposite, so they cannot be merged into one file.
- Valid example: `locks/integrity-check.pending` · Violation examples: `locks/integrity-check.lock` (read as an invalidation marker) · `locks/integrityCheck.pending` (uppercase/camel = violates lowercase plain)
- This block sets the **format** only.
<!-- anchor-end:integrity-hook-marker -->

<!-- anchor:integrity-hook-rules -->
**Integrity check hook** (`.claude/hooks/integrity-check.js`)

- Fires on = PostToolUse `Write` matcher. Registered in = `hooks.PostToolUse` of `.claude/settings.json`.
- Marker gate: it checks only when `locks/integrity-check.pending` exists, and deletes the marker right after the check. Without the marker it does nothing. Integrity marker format → grep -F "anchor:integrity-hook-marker" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
- `/pdca-wrap` creates the marker with Write after finishing all writes (that Write fires the hook).
- The check calls the same function as `check` of `nav.js`. The hook has no check logic of its own.
- systemMessage output forms (exact match · 4 states):

| State | Output regex |
|---|---|
| 0 violations | `^\[Integrity\] ✅ 0 violations( \(undetermined [0-9]+\))?$` |
| 1 or more violations | `^\[Integrity\] ❌ I-[0-9]{2}(, I-[0-9]{2})*$` |
| Format constants stale | `^\[Integrity\] ⚠ format constants stale — .+$` |
| Any other run failure | `^\[Integrity\] ⚠ check failed — .+$` |

- The hook is an auxiliary signal. `/pdca-wrap` proceeds with the verdict block that carries the values read, and with the fix loop, from the `check` output.
<!-- anchor-end:integrity-hook-rules -->

<!-- anchor:do-record-hook-rules -->
**DO record hook** (`.claude/hooks/do-check.js`)

- Fires on = Stop (when a response ends). Registered in = `hooks.Stop` of `.claude/settings.json`.
- Input = JSON on standard input. Values read = `stop_hook_active` · `transcript_path` (the transcript file · one JSON per line).
- This response = from the last user input line of the transcript file to the end of the file. User input line = a line whose `type` is `user`, whose `isSidechain` and `isMeta` are not true, and whose content has no `tool_result`.
- File-writing tools = `Write` · `Edit` · `MultiEdit` · `NotebookEdit`
- Command-running tools = `Bash` · `PowerShell`
- Target round = the round of the first `OpenRound` line of the `route` output.
- Action = a `tool_use` of this response that used a file-writing tool or a command-running tool. A file write aimed at the session file of the target round, and a command whose command string contains `nav.js`, are not counted.
- Test (the first true row from the top):

| Order | Condition | Behavior |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `stop_hook_active` is true (a response continued because this hook blocked just before) | Pass |
| 2 | There are 0 open rounds | Pass |
| 3 | A `user` line inside this response has `<command-name>/pdca-`, or this response has a `tool_use` that ran, with the `Skill` tool, a command whose name starts with `pdca-` | Pass |
| 4 | There are 0 actions | Pass |
| 5 | This response has a file-writing tool use aimed at the session file of the target round | Pass |
| 6 | Otherwise | Block |

- Pass = print nothing. Block = print a JSON whose `decision` is `block`. Its `reason` regex (exact match): `^\[DO record\] no DO entry for [0-9]+ actions — .+$`
- When a value needed for the test cannot be read (input JSON · transcript file · user input line · open round), it does not block and prints one systemMessage line. Regex (exact match): `^\[DO record\] ⚠ could not check — .+$`
- The hook only looks at whether a DO entry exists. It does not judge content or format. DO entry format → grep -F "anchor:session-file-writing" (NOTATION_CONVENTIONS)
<!-- anchor-end:do-record-hook-rules -->

